portareos-splash is vendored, not fetched.

Origin:   https://github.com/ROCKNIX/rocknix-splash
Commit:   9d295bcb74be2282e32c6b614efbea6036974ba8
License:  GPL-2.0-or-later (see ../../../../../licenses/GPL-2.0.txt)
Authors:  ROCKNIX (https://github.com/ROCKNIX). The C sources here are theirs.

Why it is vendored rather than downloaded:

The recipe used to fetch it from ROCKNIX's repository and, in practice, from
their distribution-sources mirror. Neither can serve a package named
portareos-splash, and asking them to would be wrong in any case: this is our
splash screen now, on our boot path, and it should not depend on another
project continuing to host a tarball for us.

Changed from upstream:

  Makefile  TARGET=rocknix-splash -> TARGET=portareos-splash, so the binary
            matches the name busybox/scripts/init calls. Upstream's Makefile
            hardcodes the old name, which is why renaming the package alone
            would have produced a build that succeeded and a splash that
            never appeared.

  main.c    svg_paths[] and svg_colors[] now draw the PortareOS wordmark in
            VT323 rather than the ROCKNIX logo. See the comment above the
            array for how it was generated and why it contains no Q commands.

  integrity.c
            Deleted, along with the z9() call in main.c. It was an
            obfuscated check for whether the running system is genuine
            ROCKNIX: absence of /sysroot/usr/bin/updatecheck and
            system-upgrade, an MD5 of /sysroot/etc/motd against a hardcoded
            hash, and OS_NAME plus GIT_ORGANIZATION both reading ROCKNIX in
            /sysroot/etc/os-release. When all four passed it touched
            /run/rocknix-splash.

            On this fork OS_NAME is PortareOS, so it never passed and never
            wrote the file, and nothing in the tree ever read it. What it did
            do was stat two paths, read os-release and MD5 a file on every
            boot, from obfuscated code sitting in the boot path. Removed as
            dead weight rather than as any kind of statement.

Otherwise unmodified. Fixes worth having should go upstream to ROCKNIX.
